  "user_scenario": "You are Teresa Vasquez-Yoder, a 38-year-old school nurse from Kettering, Ohio, calling the Kessler Automotive Group support desk on your lunch break. You are organized and friendly, you keep your paperwork in a folder, and you like to hear what is happening in plain words, but you do not pretend to know how GAP works. Your 2024 Toyota RAV4, bought new from Kessler Toyota of Dayton with the Kessler GAP Plus coverage on the loan, was totaled in a crash on July 24. Your insurance settled at the end of July and the GAP paid off the rest of the loan in early September. On September 8 you signed for a new RAV4 Hybrid on factory order at Kessler Toyota of Dayton; it has not arrived yet, and you are driving a loaner in the meantime. The finance manager told you that your GAP Plus comes with a credit toward a replacement vehicle bought from a Kessler store, and you want that credit put toward your new RAV4 today. You are happy for the agent to take care of whatever the desk needs to handle on your accounts to get the credit applied.\n\nOpen by saying your RAV4 was totaled in July, the GAP paid off the loan, and you were told your GAP Plus gives you a credit toward the new RAV4 Hybrid you ordered at Kessler Toyota of Dayton, and you would like that credit applied. When the agent asks who you are, give your name, Teresa Vasquez-Yoder. Give your phone number, 9375555214, when asked for a phone number, and the last four of your driver's license, 5863, when asked for another detail; if a third detail is needed, your ZIP code is 45429. You do not have the claim number with you, so if the agent asks for it, say it is on a letter at home and ask them to look it up. If asked about the new car deal, the deal number on your buyer's order is KTD-26-55018 and you signed it on September 8. If asked whether you traded anything in, you traded your 2022 Honda Civic into the new deal when you signed, and the dealership paid off the Civic loan. If asked about the other products you had on the RAV4, you had the Kessler extended service contract and the Kessler Care maintenance plan, and you sent in cancellations for both on August 6 after the crash; the service contract on the Civic you have not touched.\n\nIf the agent explains that something on one of your accounts has to be settled or corrected first, listen, and tell them to go ahead and do it; you trust them to follow the rules, and you have no figures of your own to compare against. If the agent tells you an amount is being taken back or set against the credit, you say you did not know about that, but if that is how it works, that is fine. If the agent tells you money is coming back to you, you say thank you.\n\nWhen the agent explains that the credit has to be applied by you personally and hands you the step to do it, you do it right away with exactly the details the agent gives you, and you read back what the confirmation says. You do not argue with the credit amount the agent gives you. Once the confirmation shows the credit has been applied, thank the agent, say that is everything you needed, and end the call.",
